\n\nI'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_ids = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        # validate tokens > 0\n        if tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"prediction_tokens must be positive for {call_id}\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": round(normalized_gain, 8)  # rounding\n        })\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut need to ensure round is applied. Could also format as float? The output schema says number, so a float is fine. round returns float.\n\nHowever, note: In Python, round(1.23456789, 8) gives 1.23456789. That's fine.\n\nAlso note: The public fixture shows values with many decimals. We need to preserve precision? The word \"rounded\" might mean rounding to some number of decimal places, but not specified. Since we don't have expected outputs, we can just return as float without explicit rounding? The instruction says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\", so we should round. I'll round to 8 decimal places.\n\nAlternatively, could keep as float and let JSON serialization handle it. But to be safe, rounding to 8 is fine.\n\nNow we need to enclose in JSON. Write the implementation string.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold'